# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored (`.env*` with an `!.env.example`
# exception) — do not commit real credentials.
#
# CRM-migration Step 5 (2026-10-26) removed four sections that used to be
# here — DATABASE_URL, AUTH_SECRET, CLOUDINARY_*, ADMIN_SEED_EMAIL/PASSWORD —
# along with the content database, the admin CMS, its NextAuth session layer
# and its Cloudinary upload integration they backed. This site reads and
# writes nothing of its own anymore: it is a pure client of the CRM's public
# API below. That removal did NOT touch any deployed/live database — these
# variables simply have no code left to read them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL — this site's own canonical origin
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/app/layout.tsx, robots.ts and sitemap.ts, to build an absolute
# canonical URL / the sitemap's own absolute self-reference. Predates the CRM
# migration; documented here for completeness since the end-of-migration
# report inventories every env var this build reads, not just the new ones.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because a canonical/sitemap URL is public by definition, not a
# credential.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE: falls back to "http://localhost:3000", which is
# wrong for a real deployment's canonical tags/sitemap but breaks nothing at
# build time.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://gurujitravelyatra.com"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRM_API_URL — the CRM's /api/public/* base (CRM-migration Step 1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/server/crm/config.ts. Everything BEFORE the `/public/...`
# segment — each client function in src/server/crm/client.ts appends its own
# path.
#
# Two real values, not one placeholder:
#   - Runtime (default if unset): the Railway-internal address, same project,
#     private network, no public-internet hop:
CRM_API_URL="http://gty-crm.railway.internal:8080/api"
#   - Local development against the real deployed CRM, or if the internal DNS
#     name is ever unreachable, use the public address instead:
#     CRM_API_URL="https://crm.gurujitravelyatra.com/api"
#
# ## BUILD TIME NEEDS THE PUBLIC URL — this is not optional, read before deploying
#
# Incident (2026-10-27): `next build` evaluates every statically-renderable
# page for real, which calls getSite()/getTours()/etc for real — and
# Railway's private network (where the Railway-internal address above
# actually resolves) DOES NOT EXIST during the build step, only once the
# built app is running. The build died with
# `getaddrinfo ENOTFOUND gty-crm.railway.internal` straight out of
# client.ts, because the default above is correct for RUNTIME and wrong for
# BUILD TIME — they are two different network contexts on Railway.
#
# The fix has two layers, and both matter:
#  1. CODE: src/server/crm/client.ts's withBuildPhaseFallback() now detects
#     `NEXT_PHASE === 'phase-production-build'` and returns each read's own
#     empty/"updating" fallback instead of throwing, so a misconfigured or
#     momentarily-unreachable CRM at build time no longer fails the build —
#     it produces pages that revalidate with real data on the first real
#     request instead (within the normal 60s window every read already
#     uses). This is the safety net.
#  2. CONFIG: Railway lets build-time and runtime/deploy environment
#     variables be configured SEPARATELY for the same service. Set
#     CRM_API_URL to the PUBLIC address
#     (`https://crm.gurujitravelyatra.com/api`) in Railway's BUILD-TIME
#     variable configuration specifically, and leave it unset (or set to the
#     internal address explicitly) in the RUNTIME/deploy configuration, so
#     the build fetches real data over the public internet instead of
#     silently falling back to empty pages that then have to revalidate
#     themselves after deploy. This is the preferred, intended path — layer
#     1 exists so a build still SUCCEEDS even if this step is ever missed,
#     not as a substitute for configuring it correctly.
#
# NOT a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable — every call through this client runs on the
# server (Server Components, route handlers, server actions), so the browser
# never needs this address and it is never inlined into a client bundle.
# UNSET IS SUPPORTED: it falls back to the Railway-internal address above,
# which is the right default for the (much more common) runtime case.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WEBSITE_API_KEY — the CRM's x-website-api-key for POST /public/enquiries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/server/crm/config.ts, sent ONLY on the one write endpoint this
# client calls (every GET under /public/* is @Public() and needs no key, per
# the contract). A server secret — never give this a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name, never
# log it, never echo it in a response body or a thrown error.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE, like DATABASE_URL: src/server/crm/client.ts's
# submitEnquiry() refuses to call out at all rather than sending a request
# with a missing/empty header, so local development without this set still
# runs — enquiries simply cannot be submitted until it is configured.
# WEBSITE_API_KEY="<the same value the CRM's WEBSITE_API_KEY env var holds>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOST — the CRM's R2 media origin (CRM-migration decision 4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/lib/cloudinary-url.ts. Extends the render-time media-URL
# allowlist (previously Cloudinary only) to also accept this EXACT origin —
# no wildcard, no subdomain match. NEXT_PUBLIC_ because this is a public CDN
# domain, not a credential; it is intentionally inlined into the browser
# bundle.
#
# Set it to an ORIGIN with no trailing slash and no path, e.g.:
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOST="https://media.example.r2.dev"
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE: the build does not fail, and an R2 image URL
# simply does not match the allowlist, so PhotoPlaceholder renders its
# neutral placeholder box instead of the real photo until this is set to the
# CRM's real, confirmed R2 address.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOST="https://media.example.r2.dev"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY — Cloudflare Turnstile widget (CRM-migration Step 3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/components/modals/LeadModal.tsx / TurnstileWidget.tsx. This is
# the PUBLIC half of the Turnstile key pair — safe to embed client-side, the
# same trust model as any widget sitekey (Cloudflare's own docs describe it
# as such). The SECRET half, TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY, is a CRM-side env var this
# website never reads, sends, or needs — see
# documentation/technical-specification/public-website.md's
# `POST /public/enquiries` §. The CRM verifies the token this widget
# produces against Cloudflare; this site only renders the challenge.
#
# Get a site key + secret pair at https://dash.cloudflare.com/ → Turnstile.
# The secret key goes into the CRM's OWN environment, not this one.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE, like NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOST: LeadModal does not
# render the widget at all, `turnstileToken` is never set, and "Please
# complete the verification challenge." is what a visitor sees if they try
# to submit anyway — honest, since POST /public/enquiries requires a real
# token regardless of whether this is configured.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY="<Cloudflare Turnstile site key>"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_SECRET — verifies POST /api/revalidate (CRM-migration Step 4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by src/server/crm/config.ts (getRevalidateSecret), checked against the
# incoming x-revalidate-secret header in src/app/api/revalidate/route.ts. This
# is the WEBSITE's half of a shared secret — the CRM backend holds the SAME
# value under its OWN WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_SECRET env var (contract's "Website
# revalidation hook" §) and sends it on every call. A server secret: never a
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ name, never logged.
#
# There is no WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_URL variable on THIS side — that name only
# exists in the CRM's own environment, set to this site's
# https://<this-deployment>/api/revalidate. This site never calls itself, so
# it has no use for its own URL.
#
# UNSET IS A SUPPORTED MODE, like WEBSITE_API_KEY: POST /api/revalidate
# answers 503 for every call rather than comparing against an empty string
# (which would make an empty header a false "match") — the CRM's hook is
# fire-and-forget and swallows the failure, so local development without
# this set is unaffected; content simply revalidates on the normal 60s
# window instead of immediately.
# WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_SECRET="<the same value the CRM's WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_SECRET env var holds>"
